Upstream's README.md is intentionally not vendored: it is install material,
not part of the skill.

Pre-install review (bouncer agent; SkillSpector v2.11.2 --no-llm plus a full
manual read of all 11 files; nothing executed). Verdict: CAUTION.
  - No telemetry, upload, or fetch-and-run. The only network call is a
    127.0.0.1 poll in capture_web.py, whose server binds 127.0.0.1.
  - The scanner scores it CRITICAL because the adapters launch auto-approving,
    unsandboxed agents by design: claude.sh (--dangerously-skip-permissions,
    global CLAUDE.md / skills / MCP / hooks left on), codex.sh
    (--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox), omp.sh (--auto-approve),
    opencode.sh (--auto). pi.sh passes --no-skills only.
  - The student and checks.sh inherit the caller's full environment.
  - extract_session.py redacts by regex and keeps up to 600 characters of each
    command and 400 of each tool output, so a secret in the reference session
    can reach reference.md and then the student's endpoint.
  - Operator inputs reach a shell: --setup and capture_web.py --start run with
    shell=True; the model id, --student-args and --env are expanded in run.sh;
    run paths are unquoted. --name is unvalidated and feeds an rmtree of
    runs/<task>/final/<name>.
  - `evidence --reference` runs checks.sh inside the real reference directory.
  - No hidden Unicode or injected instructions in the markdown.

Usage constraints (the `sensei` agent enforces these; follow them when using
the skill directly):
  - Local or self-hosted student endpoint only, unless the user approves a
    hosted one for that repository.
  - Do not use the claude adapter with a normal profile: the student would get
    the user's MCP connectors with permissions skipped.
  - Start trials from a shell with no credentials in the environment. The
    workspace copy is not containment.
  - Keep runs/ outside any git work tree; never commit or publish it, and do
    not publish report.html.
  - Review --setup, --env, --student-args, --name and checks.sh before each
    run; use paths and names without spaces or shell metacharacters.

